  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "High-Pass Magnitude Response",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the RC high-pass magnitude response from its frequency response and interpret how it attenuates low frequencies while passing high frequencies.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to High-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The RC high-pass response starts from the complex frequency response $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC}$. For this step, ignore phase and focus only on the magnitude, because $|H(j\\omega)|$ is the amplitude-only summary that tells us how strongly each frequency passes through the filter.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega)=\\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Taking magnitudes turns the complex ratio into a real gain formula. The move here is to read the numerator and denominator by magnitude only and simplify the result into a single expression for $|H(j\\omega)|$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This base case is the starting point for the rest of the lesson: once the magnitude formula is visible, we can read off what happens at low frequency and at high frequency, and then interpret the circuit as a high-pass filter.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing $|H(j\\omega)|$ from the High-Pass Frequency Response",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC}$, compute $|H(j\\omega)|$ and simplify it to a standard magnitude-response formula.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the complex high-pass response $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC}$. To get the magnitude response, take the absolute value of the whole fraction and separate the numerator and denominator by magnitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\left|\\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC}\\right|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The numerator has magnitude $|j\\omega RC|=\\omega RC$, and the denominator has magnitude $|1+j\\omega RC|=\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}$. That turns the complex response into a real gain formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the RC high-pass magnitude response is $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$, a real, nonnegative amplitude-only summary that we will use in the next sections to read low-frequency attenuation and high-frequency passage.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Low-Frequency Attenuation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$, determine what happens as $\\omega\\to0$ and explain why the circuit suppresses low-frequency input.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the low-frequency regime $\\omega RC\\ll1$ applied to the magnitude formula $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$. That tells us to use the same amplitude-only response from the previous step and simplify it at small $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When $\\omega RC\\ll1$, the term $(\\omega RC)^2$ is tiny, so $\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}\\approx1$. The gain therefore becomes proportional to $\\omega$, which is exactly the low-frequency attenuation behavior we want to recognize.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}|H(j\\omega)|&=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}\\\\&\\approx\\frac{\\omega RC}{1}\\\\&=\\omega RC\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\omega\\to0}|H(j\\omega)|=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the response shrinks to zero as frequency goes to zero, which means the RC high-pass filter strongly attenuates low-frequency input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting High-Frequency Passage",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$, determine what happens as $\\omega\\to\\infty$ and explain why the circuit passes high-frequency input.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the high-frequency regime $\\omega RC\\gg1$ applied to the same magnitude formula $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$. That tells us to check what the ratio becomes when the frequency is much larger than the cutoff scale.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When $\\omega RC\\gg1$, both the numerator and the dominant term in the denominator scale like $\\omega RC$. So the gain approaches $1$ rather than shrinking toward $0$, which is the signature of high-frequency passage.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}|H(j\\omega)|&=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}\\\\&\\approx\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\omega RC}\\\\&=1\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}|H(j\\omega)|=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So for very large $\\omega$, the RC high-pass filter passes the input with near-unity gain, which means the high-frequency part of the signal comes through essentially unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reading the High-Pass Magnitude Response as a Filter",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Using $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$, explain why the RC circuit passes high frequencies while attenuating low frequencies.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is to combine the two endpoint facts from the same magnitude response. We already know how the response behaves near $\\omega=0$ and as $\\omega\\to\\infty$, so the task is to turn those limits into a filter classification.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}|H(j\\omega)|&\\to0 \\quad \\text{as } \\omega\\to0\\\\|H(j\\omega)|&\\to1 \\quad \\text{as } \\omega\\to\\infty\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That means low-frequency components are strongly attenuated because their output magnitude is near zero, while high-frequency components are passed because their output magnitude is near one. The magnitude response separates the input by frequency content rather than treating all frequencies alike.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the RC circuit is a high-pass filter: the magnitude response suppresses low frequencies and passes high frequencies.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
